## Your task
Calibrate each condition and the outcome that is marked "YOU CALIBRATE". Do not mention fixed sets in your answer. There are two ways a set can be calibrated:

Three anchors (direct calibration), on the RAW measurement scale:
- full_non_membership: the raw value at or beyond which a case is clearly NOT in the set (membership about 0.05)
- crossover: the raw value of maximum ambiguity, where a case is neither more in nor more out of the set (membership 0.50)
- full_membership: the raw value at or beyond which a case is clearly IN the set (membership about 0.95)
For a set whose membership runs with the raw scale, full_non_membership < crossover < full_membership.
For a set whose membership runs opposite to the raw scale, the order is reversed. Anchors must lie within the
observed range shown above.

Three breakpoints (four-value calibration), on the RAW measurement scale:
- break_0: the boundary between score 0 and score 0.33. A case at or beyond it (on the side of less membership) scores 0 (fully out). It may lie just outside the observed range if you judge that no case is fully out.
- break_33: the boundary between score 0.33 and score 0.67 (cases that only minimally or partly meet the idea of the set, versus cases that substantially meet it)
- break_67: the boundary between score 0.67 and score 1 (cases that substantially meet it, versus cases that fully meet it)
For a set whose membership runs with the raw scale: break_0 < break_33 < break_67, and a case exactly on a boundary takes the LOWER of the two scores. For a set whose membership runs opposite to the raw scale the order is reversed. break_33 and break_67 must lie within the observed range shown above.

Write each rationale as two to four sentences of substantive reasoning. A good rationale:
(a) says what a case at that raw value looks like in substance, using the construct definition and the wording or
scale points of the measurement instrument;
(b) explains why the anchor or breakpoint is not noticeably lower or higher, that is, what would change in the case's standing in the set;
(c) reflects the vantage point you were given, if any, and says what a different reader might see differently;
(d) uses the observed distribution only as an empirical check on plausibility, never as the reason for the value.
A rationale that merely restates a percentile, the shape of the distribution, or the definition of the anchor is not acceptable.

Also choose the truth-table cutoffs, each with a substantive rationale of the same kind:
- consistency_threshold in [0,1]: the minimum raw consistency for a combination of conditions to count as sufficient for the outcome. Explain how much inconsistency you consider tolerable given measurement error and the nature of these cases.
- frequency_threshold (integer >= 1): the minimum number of cases for a combination to be considered. Explain how many cases you consider enough evidence given the number of cases and conditions in this study.
- pri_threshold (optional, or null): if you set one, say what it guards against.

